00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:37.880
So most of the
customers, before they

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:40.010
start using Cloud
Dataflow, they use

00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:43.140
something that is called
a lambda architecture.

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:46.600
A collection of tools,
Abobe Spark, Kafka,

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:50.120
which is optimized for
specific flows of data

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:54.030
for batch processing and
for steaming processing.

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:57.890
Once our customers are
switching to data flow,

00:01:57.890 --> 00:02:00.580
they take advantage
of a unified model.

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:04.010
This is one of the major
reasons why customers are

00:02:04.010 --> 00:02:06.590
migrating to Cloud Dataflow--

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:10.220
it offers a unified way of
doing batch and steaming,

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:11.750
and you can use
it in conjunction

00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:16.850
with other GCP-assisted products
such as Pub/Sub and BigQuery

00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:20.720
to build your data
processing pipelines.

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:25.010
If you're a fan of open-source
computing-- and who isn't--

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:29.690
you can also use Cloud Dataflow
with another stack of data

00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:31.390
processing applications.

00:02:31.390 --> 00:02:34.600
We have open-sourced
our SDK and it

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:38.120
became Apache Beam, which
just recently became

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:40.620
a top-level Apache project.

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:43.410
And you can use beam
runners on a variety--

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:46.940
in a variety of environments,
including Spark, Fling,

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:50.600
and some others.

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:53.570
To summarize, why customers
are using Cloud Dataflow.

00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:56.900
By the way, just
by a show of hands,

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:02.060
who has already been able to
run a few data flow pipelines?

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:03.740
OK good.

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:07.180
Maybe a third, a
quarter of the audience.

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:09.290
So Cloud Dataflow
offers you a unified way

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:11.740
of doing batch and steaming.

00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:13.880
You get access to a fully
managed environment,

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:17.840
so you can spend your time
on optimizing your pipeline

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:23.000
code instead of running and
managing server infrastructure.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:25.530
We offer you a open-source
programming model,

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:28.010
so you are not locked
in to just one vendor.

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:31.980
You can run Beam pipelines
in Cloud Dataflow,

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:33.590
or you can run and--

00:03:33.590 --> 00:03:36.110
run it on premises
or other cloud

00:03:36.110 --> 00:03:39.950
providers with other runners
such as Spark and Flint.

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:43.940
And we are also able
to scale from maybe 10

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:46.220
records per second
incoming data stream

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:51.190
to millions of
records per second.

00:03:51.190 --> 00:03:53.940
Architecturally, here's
an architechtural overview

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:56.470
of Cloud Dataflow.

00:03:56.470 --> 00:04:01.120
We are a black box that
connects sources and sinks.

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:03.790
We support streaming
and batch sources.

00:04:03.790 --> 00:04:06.280
We call them
bounded-unbounded sources,

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:08.170
and we write into
a variety of sinks.

00:04:08.170 --> 00:04:11.030
So we offer connectors
for sources and sinks.

00:04:11.030 --> 00:04:15.310
We also offer a bunch of
benefits under the hood,

00:04:15.310 --> 00:04:18.670
including-- the create the
worker VMs for you will,

00:04:18.670 --> 00:04:21.200
which actually execute
and run your code.

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:25.090
We optimize the pipelines
that you submit to us.

00:04:25.090 --> 00:04:31.230
You can keep kind of thinking
at a more abstract level.

00:04:31.230 --> 00:04:35.170
And if you are a SQL programmer,
think about writing a SQL query

00:04:35.170 --> 00:04:37.660
and then submitting
it to a database

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:40.142
and we do the
optimizations for you.

00:04:40.142 --> 00:04:44.440
We scale resources
for you, up and down,

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:45.670
depending on the workload.

00:04:45.670 --> 00:04:49.630
And we do a redistribution
of work between workers when

00:04:49.630 --> 00:04:53.670
particular workers become hot.

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:56.030
I'm going to talk about
two specific benefits later

00:04:56.030 --> 00:04:57.640
in my presentation.

00:04:57.640 --> 00:05:00.120
But that was an overview
of all the benefits

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:01.850
that Dataflow provides.

00:05:01.850 --> 00:05:04.520
If you are a developer, then
from a developer's point

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:06.970
of view you write pipeline code.

00:05:06.970 --> 00:05:08.880
We support multiple languages--

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:11.690
Java and Python.

00:05:11.690 --> 00:05:18.650
This code has access to a wide
variety of data transformation

00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:21.830
functions in our SDKs.

00:05:21.830 --> 00:05:24.450
The SDKs are
language independent,

00:05:24.450 --> 00:05:26.690
so they offer the
same functionality

00:05:26.690 --> 00:05:29.680
in both Java and Python.

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:31.430
We call it the Beam model.

00:05:31.430 --> 00:05:33.030
Beam provides abstractions.

00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:36.290
For example, if you
want to do parallel--

00:05:36.290 --> 00:05:39.350
to the process data
elements in parallel.

00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:42.080
You can use a
transform called ParDo.

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:43.230
We call ParDos the--

00:05:43.230 --> 00:05:45.800
our transform for
parallel processing.

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:49.130
If you want to group things
by key in any language--

00:05:49.130 --> 00:05:53.190
Java or Python, you use
a group by key transform.

00:05:53.190 --> 00:05:56.690
And as I already mentioned,
once you wrote your pipeline

00:05:56.690 --> 00:06:00.680
and kind of chose your
language-- your SDK--

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:03.380
you can run it in a
variety of runners.

00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:06.160
You can run it in
Cloud Dataflow.

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:09.296
You can run it in Spark,
and you can run it in Flint.

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:15.560
So I wanted to highlight four
specific technical benefits

00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:18.600
of Cloud Dataflow before
I do my first demo.

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:21.611
I already mentioned the
full management aspect

00:06:21.611 --> 00:06:22.360
of Cloud Dataflow.

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:24.080
We create workers for you.

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:28.910
We allocate-- we split your
data inputs into smaller shards,

00:06:28.910 --> 00:06:30.245
and allocate them to workers.

00:06:32.770 --> 00:06:35.750
We also do optimizations
of your execution code.

00:06:35.750 --> 00:06:38.150
For example, you might
start with a pipeline,

00:06:38.150 --> 00:06:40.950
and pipelines can be
thought of activity diagrams

00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:45.080
where you have bubbles of
transformations and data

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:47.570
flowing from one
transformation bubble

00:06:47.570 --> 00:06:49.940
to the next
transformation bubble.

00:06:49.940 --> 00:06:52.590
So you think in terms of
these activity diagrams.

00:06:52.590 --> 00:06:54.050
And what you want
to do with data,

00:06:54.050 --> 00:06:57.230
you define your transforms
and you connect them.

00:06:57.230 --> 00:07:00.260
You submit your
pipeline graphs to us

00:07:00.260 --> 00:07:02.730
and we optimize the execution.

00:07:02.730 --> 00:07:05.390
Sometimes we will
merge steps together

00:07:05.390 --> 00:07:09.830
if we think that it will
be more efficient to do

00:07:09.830 --> 00:07:11.930
both transformations together.

00:07:11.930 --> 00:07:15.880
Sometimes we split them up
to achieve a higher level

00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:19.430
of parallelism.

00:07:19.430 --> 00:07:23.150
Yet another benefit of Cloud
Dataflow is autoscaling.

00:07:23.150 --> 00:07:25.190
So think about a
workload which maybe

00:07:25.190 --> 00:07:30.502
starts with just 800 records
per second incoming data stream.

00:07:30.502 --> 00:07:32.210
It's the beginning of
the day, your users

00:07:32.210 --> 00:07:34.670
are not doing much,
so just generating

00:07:34.670 --> 00:07:38.020
about 8,000 records per second.

00:07:38.020 --> 00:07:39.930
We'll allocate just
a sufficient number

00:07:39.930 --> 00:07:45.180
of workers which are required
to process in incoming work

00:07:45.180 --> 00:07:46.280
stream.

00:07:46.280 --> 00:07:48.680
Now during the day,
your users will

00:07:48.680 --> 00:07:51.470
begin submitting more events.

00:07:51.470 --> 00:07:53.500
It might reach
several thousand--

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:56.630
5,000 in this example.

00:07:56.630 --> 00:07:58.670
Through autoscaling,
Cloud Dataflow

00:07:58.670 --> 00:08:03.410
is able to sense the increase
in CPU utilization, the increase

00:08:03.410 --> 00:08:07.310
in incoming data rates, and
increase the number of workers

00:08:07.310 --> 00:08:10.280
automatically a much
training number.

00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:12.350
Not only do we increase
the number of workers,

00:08:12.350 --> 00:08:13.340
we also decrease them.

00:08:13.340 --> 00:08:15.830
And the workload is--

00:08:15.830 --> 00:08:19.130
when your incoming data
stream is declining.

00:08:19.130 --> 00:08:22.149
And lastly, the last feature
I wanted to highlight.

00:08:22.149 --> 00:08:24.440
The are the two features--
autoscaling and dynamic load

00:08:24.440 --> 00:08:26.640
rebalancing-- which I
wanted to kind of deep dive

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:27.770
a little bit later.

00:08:27.770 --> 00:08:31.250
It's important that I cover
them on a higher level first.

00:08:31.250 --> 00:08:34.460
Is dynamic work rebalancing.

00:08:34.460 --> 00:08:38.450
I already mentioned that when
you tell us your data inputs

00:08:38.450 --> 00:08:43.429
and let us allocate the workers
to process these data inputs,

00:08:43.429 --> 00:08:46.420
some of the data shards
which we create--

00:08:46.420 --> 00:08:48.970
data shards of your input data--

00:08:48.970 --> 00:08:53.120
might contain data which
takes longer to process

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:56.120
than all the other shards.

00:08:56.120 --> 00:09:00.130
We call such shards stragglers.

00:09:00.130 --> 00:09:04.100
And sometimes-- well
actually, quite often--

00:09:04.100 --> 00:09:06.230
because of the
existence of stragglers,

00:09:06.230 --> 00:09:10.370
your entire data
pipeline will take longer

00:09:10.370 --> 00:09:11.780
to execute because
we are waiting

00:09:11.780 --> 00:09:15.120
for the completion
of the straggler.

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:17.700
With this feature that we
have, dynamic work rebalancing,

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:20.390
we are actually able to
steal, take away work

00:09:20.390 --> 00:09:22.850
from the worker
which is struggling

00:09:22.850 --> 00:09:25.650
and reallocate it
to other workers.

00:09:25.650 --> 00:09:29.640
Now I will show it in a demo,
which I want to do just now.

00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:31.370
Can we switch to the
demo show, please.

00:09:41.979 --> 00:09:43.520
First, in the demo
I'm going to start

00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:49.142
with showing you how easy it is
to start a data flow pipeline.

00:09:49.142 --> 00:09:50.600
We have recently
launched a feature

00:09:50.600 --> 00:09:52.340
called Dataflow Templates.

00:09:52.340 --> 00:09:55.180
Templates allow you to
write your pipeline,

00:09:55.180 --> 00:10:00.200
deposit it into a GCS bucket,
and then call on this code

00:10:00.200 --> 00:10:02.780
whenever you would like and
wherever you would like.

00:10:02.780 --> 00:10:07.010
You can initiate this
template from the console.

00:10:07.010 --> 00:10:10.520
You can initiate this
template into a data flow job

00:10:10.520 --> 00:10:14.000
from GCloud or through the API.

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:18.470
In my demo I'm going to use a
export off a publicly available

00:10:18.470 --> 00:10:20.600
data set of Hacker News.

00:10:20.600 --> 00:10:24.200
Those of you who know
me, know that I am very

00:10:24.200 --> 00:10:25.820
excited about text analytics.

00:10:25.820 --> 00:10:28.180
I love natural
language processing.

00:10:28.180 --> 00:10:32.990
I write algorithms for
crunching through news articles,

00:10:32.990 --> 00:10:35.870
trying to extract
opinions from text.

00:10:35.870 --> 00:10:37.370
So I took the--

00:10:37.370 --> 00:10:40.280
I took one of the public
data sets that we have,

00:10:40.280 --> 00:10:45.170
Hacker News news posts, and
dumped them into a bunch

00:10:45.170 --> 00:10:48.070
of .csv files for this demo.

00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:57.550
As you can see in this GCS
bucket where I dumped my Hacker

00:10:57.550 --> 00:11:04.830
News data set, I have about
30 or 40 .csv files of a size

00:11:04.830 --> 00:11:07.375
of about 125 megabytes.

00:11:07.375 --> 00:11:11.010
But one particular
file is extra long,

00:11:11.010 --> 00:11:13.590
and you'll see why
it's important.

00:11:13.590 --> 00:11:19.252
This file, a very large file
.csv is 5.5 gigabytes long.

00:11:19.252 --> 00:11:21.210
And those are the stragglers
I'm talking about.

00:11:21.210 --> 00:11:23.730
Sometimes your data
is just larger--

00:11:23.730 --> 00:11:27.030
your data shards are larger
than other data shards.

00:11:27.030 --> 00:11:29.536
So let me started my
Dataflow pipeline.

00:11:35.250 --> 00:11:39.530
I'm going to go to the Dataflow
console and click on run job.

00:11:42.330 --> 00:11:45.270
In this new UI, you are able
to choose which template

00:11:45.270 --> 00:11:47.340
you would like to instantiate.

00:11:47.340 --> 00:11:48.660
Some of the options are--

00:11:48.660 --> 00:11:50.190
I can do a custom template.

00:11:50.190 --> 00:11:55.560
Custom template is the code
that you write and place in GCS.

00:11:55.560 --> 00:11:57.870
And you can then
share it with others

00:11:57.870 --> 00:12:01.650
and stantiate it into
Dataflow jobs, etc.

00:12:01.650 --> 00:12:04.470
I will actually use
a simple algorithm

00:12:04.470 --> 00:12:06.150
that we typically do for--

00:12:06.150 --> 00:12:08.550
typically share with
customers when they

00:12:08.550 --> 00:12:10.530
start learning about data flow.

00:12:10.530 --> 00:12:14.190
It's the word count algorithm.

00:12:14.190 --> 00:12:15.150
It's quite simple.

00:12:15.150 --> 00:12:18.210
It reads from text files,
counts the number of words,

00:12:18.210 --> 00:12:23.120
and then does statistics
on these words.

00:12:23.120 --> 00:12:24.560
I'll give the job a name--

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:31.060
demojob01, and
specify my input--

00:12:31.060 --> 00:12:31.910
GCS bucket.

00:12:45.740 --> 00:12:51.860
I'll use a pattern to catch all
the .csv files in this bucket.

00:12:51.860 --> 00:12:56.440
And lastly, I will also
specify my output bucket

00:12:56.440 --> 00:12:59.990
where the text statistics
should eventually end up.

00:13:07.090 --> 00:13:10.270
That's all I needed to do
to launch a data processing

00:13:10.270 --> 00:13:11.200
pipeline.

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:13.290
I didn't have to
install any IDEs,

00:13:13.290 --> 00:13:16.210
I didn't have to launch VMs.

00:13:16.210 --> 00:13:19.310
I can basically
launch now pipelines

00:13:19.310 --> 00:13:22.833
through the console using
the templating mechanism.

00:13:27.930 --> 00:13:29.830
And within seconds,
Dataflow will

00:13:29.830 --> 00:13:33.310
start showing you the execution
steps of this pipeline.

00:13:33.310 --> 00:13:35.690
As I mentioned before,
it's relatively simple.

00:13:35.690 --> 00:13:38.340
It reads lines from
my input files.

00:13:38.340 --> 00:13:41.710
It runs the text
calculation algorithms.

00:13:41.710 --> 00:13:44.870
It does some mapping work,
and then it [INAUDIBLE]

00:13:44.870 --> 00:13:46.860
into GCS files.

00:13:46.860 --> 00:13:50.680
I will come back to this
pipeline in about 10 minutes,

00:13:50.680 --> 00:13:53.380
to show you a couple
of other things

00:13:53.380 --> 00:13:57.730
that I will continue talking
about during my presentation.

00:13:57.730 --> 00:13:58.517
Slides please.

00:14:04.860 --> 00:14:06.840
So this is the code
of the pipeline.

00:14:06.840 --> 00:14:11.720
Reading from files,
applying a data transform

00:14:11.720 --> 00:14:16.410
to calculate statistics
on text, in parallel form

00:14:16.410 --> 00:14:20.540
of the outputs, and
writing back into files.

00:14:20.540 --> 00:14:24.890
Because Cloud Dataflow is
a parallel data processing

00:14:24.890 --> 00:14:28.310
service, we offer you
as a user the ability

00:14:28.310 --> 00:14:31.640
to run your transformation--
data transformations

00:14:31.640 --> 00:14:32.750
in parallel.

00:14:32.750 --> 00:14:35.220
And we call these
capabilities ParDos.

00:14:35.220 --> 00:14:38.360
So basically, define
a transform function,

00:14:38.360 --> 00:14:40.470
we call them Do functions--

00:14:40.470 --> 00:14:44.480
DoFuns-- and then
you tell the ParDo

00:14:44.480 --> 00:14:50.330
transform to go and
execute your Do function.

00:14:50.330 --> 00:14:53.690
The Do function takes
data in a certain shape--

00:14:53.690 --> 00:14:57.200
in this diagram it is
represented by yellow bricks

00:14:57.200 --> 00:15:00.390
or yellow squares--

00:15:00.390 --> 00:15:04.700
and reshapes them into data
elements of a different shape.

00:15:04.700 --> 00:15:09.830
Maybe adds additional fields,
removes them, aggregates them.

00:15:09.830 --> 00:15:14.110
Again in the diagram,
represented as green stars.

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:21.900
Since Cloud Dataflow is a
parallel data processing

00:15:21.900 --> 00:15:26.370
service, we run many,
many workers that do--

00:15:26.370 --> 00:15:29.070
that execute your transforms.

00:15:29.070 --> 00:15:33.990
The best way of visualizing
what your workers are doing--

00:15:33.990 --> 00:15:36.240
your worker virtual
machines are doing

00:15:36.240 --> 00:15:38.880
is to display a Gantt chart.

00:15:38.880 --> 00:15:43.620
In my diagram here, I have eight
workers working on data shards.

00:15:43.620 --> 00:15:44.980
Data shards are my--

00:15:44.980 --> 00:15:49.860
is my input data set that I
subdivided into smaller chunks.

00:15:49.860 --> 00:15:52.740
So these eight workers
take a data shard.

00:15:52.740 --> 00:15:54.930
They work on it,
they complete it,

00:15:54.930 --> 00:15:59.460
and they start working
on the next shard.

00:15:59.460 --> 00:16:04.930
Here's a real-life Gantt
chart of a data flow pipeline

00:16:04.930 --> 00:16:11.300
of 400 workers running for about
20 minutes doing three things.

00:16:11.300 --> 00:16:16.290
It reads files, it then does
a group by key operation.

00:16:16.290 --> 00:16:19.230
And this is why you see this
way-- a clean separation

00:16:19.230 --> 00:16:20.470
between stages.

00:16:20.470 --> 00:16:22.140
First it needs to
read the files.

00:16:22.140 --> 00:16:24.820
Once it has the
entire data set, it

00:16:24.820 --> 00:16:28.770
begins grouping data by keys.

00:16:28.770 --> 00:16:30.150
And it also writes into files.

00:16:34.740 --> 00:16:36.870
With parallel data
processing, one

00:16:36.870 --> 00:16:38.790
of the big problems
that I mentioned before

00:16:38.790 --> 00:16:43.350
is the presence of stragglers.

00:16:43.350 --> 00:16:46.380
And stragglers are
really your data shards

00:16:46.380 --> 00:16:50.220
which take longer to execute
than the average execution

00:16:50.220 --> 00:16:54.260
time for all the other data
shards of your data set.

00:16:54.260 --> 00:16:59.610
In this diagram here, stragglers
would be the bar in red,

00:16:59.610 --> 00:17:04.440
and the yellow one is kind
of approaching the cutoff.

00:17:07.140 --> 00:17:08.540
Where do stragglers come from?

00:17:08.540 --> 00:17:11.430
And this is important, because
if we want to provide you

00:17:11.430 --> 00:17:14.280
a service which does--

00:17:14.280 --> 00:17:17.329
minimizes the resources
that we utilize,

00:17:17.329 --> 00:17:20.930
that improves the execution
performance of your data

00:17:20.930 --> 00:17:25.540
pipelines, we need to be
able to detect stragglers

00:17:25.540 --> 00:17:28.420
and we need to be able
to deal with them.

00:17:28.420 --> 00:17:30.150
So the first step
here is to understand

00:17:30.150 --> 00:17:32.250
where they could come from.

00:17:32.250 --> 00:17:35.550
They could be coming from
an uneven distribution

00:17:35.550 --> 00:17:37.710
of your input data.

00:17:37.710 --> 00:17:41.100
For example, if
your input data sets

00:17:41.100 --> 00:17:46.290
are textual files and speak
your favorite language--

00:17:46.290 --> 00:17:49.110
let's say English.

00:17:49.110 --> 00:17:53.320
English has a natural
skewed data distribution

00:17:53.320 --> 00:17:56.100
to the frequency
of words starting

00:17:56.100 --> 00:17:58.590
with a particular letter.

00:17:58.590 --> 00:18:02.580
So all the data shards
which contain words

00:18:02.580 --> 00:18:05.640
starting with a T, for
example, will be longer

00:18:05.640 --> 00:18:08.940
than other data shards.

00:18:08.940 --> 00:18:10.700
Where else do data shards--

00:18:10.700 --> 00:18:12.760
stragglers come from?

00:18:12.760 --> 00:18:15.690
Well for example, if
you do a join operation

00:18:15.690 --> 00:18:17.840
and you have to--

00:18:17.840 --> 00:18:19.940
you have two input data sets.

00:18:19.940 --> 00:18:24.120
And you are trying to join
each key on the left hand side,

00:18:24.120 --> 00:18:27.150
with the responding keys
on the right hand side.

00:18:27.150 --> 00:18:29.010
For some of the
keys on the left--

00:18:29.010 --> 00:18:31.980
I'm using the left-right
terminology in joins--

00:18:31.980 --> 00:18:35.880
some of the keys on the left,
you will have many more records

00:18:35.880 --> 00:18:37.260
on the right.

00:18:37.260 --> 00:18:43.600
And this is again another reason
you could have stragglers.

00:18:43.600 --> 00:18:46.320
Not only does the data or--

00:18:46.320 --> 00:18:48.970
yes, data could kind
of lead to straddlers.

00:18:48.970 --> 00:18:52.960
You can sometimes also have
issues with the infrastructure.

00:18:52.960 --> 00:18:56.140
And you can have issues
with networking, or perhaps

00:18:56.140 --> 00:18:57.430
a slow desk.

00:18:57.430 --> 00:19:02.330
Something is going on
with the OS or the VM.

00:19:02.330 --> 00:19:05.290
There are not enough resources
on a particular virtual machine

00:19:05.290 --> 00:19:09.150
that runs your code to
process data quickly.

00:19:09.150 --> 00:19:12.640
It's yet another reason
for data stragglers.

00:19:12.640 --> 00:19:15.340
What could you do to
address this issue?

00:19:15.340 --> 00:19:19.650
Well you could start splitting
your input data into yet--

00:19:19.650 --> 00:19:22.690
a high number of shards.

00:19:22.690 --> 00:19:26.380
That might help in some cases.

00:19:26.380 --> 00:19:29.710
You can try to
alert your users--

00:19:29.710 --> 00:19:35.260
you being the vendor of a
data processing service.

00:19:35.260 --> 00:19:38.710
You could let your users
define business rules

00:19:38.710 --> 00:19:43.540
and try to kind of fine tune
how many workers are allocated

00:19:43.540 --> 00:19:46.290
to each shards
which of the workers

00:19:46.290 --> 00:19:49.360
are being allocated to
the shards, and so on.

00:19:49.360 --> 00:19:52.330
That's a little complex,
but can be done.

00:19:52.330 --> 00:19:55.180
You could collect
statistics on data

00:19:55.180 --> 00:19:57.270
before the execution
of the pipeline,

00:19:57.270 --> 00:20:02.560
and create and start your
stragglers first, for example,

00:20:02.560 --> 00:20:06.220
so that they can
complete faster.

00:20:06.220 --> 00:20:09.640
On the hardware side, or
the infrastructure side--

00:20:09.640 --> 00:20:12.430
this is especially something
that folks in the Hadoop world

00:20:12.430 --> 00:20:13.480
do--

00:20:13.480 --> 00:20:20.207
could do backups and restarts,
which help with this problem.

00:20:20.207 --> 00:20:21.790
Well at Cloud Dataflow,
we spend a lot

00:20:21.790 --> 00:20:23.590
of time thinking
about these problems.

00:20:23.590 --> 00:20:28.390
And we deal with them by
first detecting stragglers

00:20:28.390 --> 00:20:29.860
and fighting.

00:20:29.860 --> 00:20:35.630
Fighting meaning dealing
with this problem.

00:20:35.630 --> 00:20:37.870
So the first step is
to identify stragglers.

00:20:37.870 --> 00:20:39.230
So what do we do?

00:20:39.230 --> 00:20:41.560
For each of the shards,
and each of the workers

00:20:41.560 --> 00:20:44.050
which process these
shards, we always

00:20:44.050 --> 00:20:47.230
track the estimated
completion time.

00:20:47.230 --> 00:20:49.060
We always estimate
how long it will

00:20:49.060 --> 00:20:52.750
take to finish the processing
of an active shard.

00:20:52.750 --> 00:20:56.620
And when we see that
the expected completion

00:20:56.620 --> 00:21:00.100
time exceeds the
average completion

00:21:00.100 --> 00:21:03.910
time for all the other
shards, we raise a flag.

00:21:03.910 --> 00:21:05.980
This is a indicator for us--

00:21:05.980 --> 00:21:08.080
this shard will
become a straggler

00:21:08.080 --> 00:21:11.620
and will slow down the pipeline.

00:21:11.620 --> 00:21:12.460
So what do we do?

00:21:12.460 --> 00:21:14.200
We split them up.

00:21:14.200 --> 00:21:16.810
Splitting up means we
ask the worker which

00:21:16.810 --> 00:21:21.220
is responsible for processing
these stragglers to give back

00:21:21.220 --> 00:21:24.180
some of the work that
they are responsible for.

00:21:24.180 --> 00:21:27.790
The giving back
could consist from,

00:21:27.790 --> 00:21:33.640
give me a range of line
items in your input data set.

00:21:33.640 --> 00:21:36.040
Give me a particular
IDs in your input data

00:21:36.040 --> 00:21:39.490
set that you are unable
to process quickly,

00:21:39.490 --> 00:21:42.940
so that I can reallocate
it to other workers.

00:21:45.710 --> 00:21:47.180
So we continuously do that.

00:21:47.180 --> 00:21:49.570
And in my diagram
here, the green bars

00:21:49.570 --> 00:21:52.160
represent work that
is already completed.

00:21:52.160 --> 00:21:58.190
I have eight workers, have
already done nine shards.

00:21:58.190 --> 00:22:00.700
The fourth worker
devoted to [INAUDIBLE].

00:22:00.700 --> 00:22:03.540
The yellow bars represent the
shards they are currently,

00:22:03.540 --> 00:22:08.990
actively working on
and the beige bars

00:22:08.990 --> 00:22:12.410
represent the estimated
completion time.

00:22:12.410 --> 00:22:15.320
And for each of these
shards, we have calculated

00:22:15.320 --> 00:22:20.150
the estimated completion time
and we detected for two of them

00:22:20.150 --> 00:22:21.620
that they will be late.

00:22:21.620 --> 00:22:24.700
And so we will try to
allocate the work--

00:22:24.700 --> 00:22:26.810
we will not try, we will
reallocate the work.

00:22:26.810 --> 00:22:28.940
We will take away
work from these shards

00:22:28.940 --> 00:22:32.210
and reassign it
to other workers.

00:22:32.210 --> 00:22:35.870
Not only do it do it do it
once during the execution

00:22:35.870 --> 00:22:40.640
of the pipeline, we
do this repeatedly.

00:22:40.640 --> 00:22:43.530
And here's what it
actually leads to.

00:22:43.530 --> 00:22:47.490
Going back to my example
of a real-life pipeline--

00:22:47.490 --> 00:22:51.400
it's actually an example
I haven't shown yet.

00:22:51.400 --> 00:22:55.625
The pipeline is a relatively
simple transformation.

00:22:55.625 --> 00:22:59.470
It does only one type of
transformation on your data,

00:22:59.470 --> 00:23:01.960
and it runs on 24 workers.

00:23:01.960 --> 00:23:06.430
I'm talking about the diagram
in the upper left quadrant here.

00:23:06.430 --> 00:23:10.440
So 24 workers trying to do a
very simple transformation,

00:23:10.440 --> 00:23:12.280
a very simple ParDo.

00:23:12.280 --> 00:23:15.140
But there's a skewed
data distribution,

00:23:15.140 --> 00:23:21.190
and two of these shards will
take much longer than the rest.

00:23:21.190 --> 00:23:25.030
Without load rebalancing, the
execution of this pipeline

00:23:25.030 --> 00:23:27.880
will take nine minutes.

00:23:27.880 --> 00:23:31.390
But look what load rebalancing
did to this pipeline.

00:23:31.390 --> 00:23:35.560
It reduced the execution
time almost 50%,

00:23:35.560 --> 00:23:38.830
to about four minutes
and 20 seconds.

00:23:38.830 --> 00:23:43.000
And look how much more
utilized your workers are now.

00:23:43.000 --> 00:23:45.050
They complete at
approximately the same time.

00:23:47.860 --> 00:23:49.950
Here's another
example of a pipeline

00:23:49.950 --> 00:23:53.100
that I mentioned previously.

00:23:53.100 --> 00:23:58.990
400 workers doing somewhat
more involved transformations.

00:23:58.990 --> 00:24:03.270
A read ParDo, so
reading from files.

00:24:03.270 --> 00:24:07.170
Doing a group by key,
writing to files.

00:24:07.170 --> 00:24:08.640
The data is not the problem--

00:24:08.640 --> 00:24:11.000
data is uniformly distributed.

00:24:11.000 --> 00:24:15.090
But there is still a
bit of lag between some

00:24:15.090 --> 00:24:17.190
of the workers in
the reading part

00:24:17.190 --> 00:24:23.560
and some of the variation in
the workers in the writing part.

00:24:23.560 --> 00:24:27.810
Without load rebalancing, the
pipeline takes 20 minutes.

00:24:27.810 --> 00:24:33.660
With load rebalancing,
the savings are not huge,

00:24:33.660 --> 00:24:36.270
but they are nice 25%.

00:24:36.270 --> 00:24:39.120
Utilization is
significantly higher,

00:24:39.120 --> 00:24:41.040
and we've reduced
all the slack--

00:24:41.040 --> 00:24:43.320
all the white space
between the workers.

00:24:47.510 --> 00:24:51.400
Dynamic load balancing
is great in isolation,

00:24:51.400 --> 00:24:53.470
but it is especially
is useful when

00:24:53.470 --> 00:24:56.320
you start thinking
about adjusting

00:24:56.320 --> 00:25:03.430
your workload to the variations
in the incoming data stream.

00:25:03.430 --> 00:25:06.610
What we call autoscale.

00:25:06.610 --> 00:25:11.110
Think about a case where you
start with three workers.

00:25:11.110 --> 00:25:14.380
That's my diagram up
in the first row here.

00:25:14.380 --> 00:25:19.510
So three workers working
on several data shards.

00:25:19.510 --> 00:25:22.150
And for the first
couple of minutes,

00:25:22.150 --> 00:25:25.900
since we are doing estimation
of the completion time,

00:25:25.900 --> 00:25:27.140
everything looks fine.

00:25:27.140 --> 00:25:31.090
It looks like we can process
the data within 10 minutes.

00:25:31.090 --> 00:25:32.830
Not a big deal.

00:25:32.830 --> 00:25:36.700
But as time progresses, and
as data flow is consistently

00:25:36.700 --> 00:25:40.900
and continuously re-estimating
the completion time,

00:25:40.900 --> 00:25:44.620
we learn more about
the input data sets.

00:25:44.620 --> 00:25:49.930
We begin sensing that the actual
completion will be much longer.

00:25:49.930 --> 00:25:53.740
The first couple of records in
our data charts were atypical,

00:25:53.740 --> 00:25:56.200
and the average record
in our data shard

00:25:56.200 --> 00:26:00.580
will cause the pipeline
to take 3 days.

00:26:00.580 --> 00:26:01.120
Great.

00:26:01.120 --> 00:26:03.220
So we realize we
could definitely

00:26:03.220 --> 00:26:05.680
use some additional help.

00:26:05.680 --> 00:26:10.330
Why don't we start 100
new workers and crunch

00:26:10.330 --> 00:26:14.020
through the data just by
throwing more CPU on it?

00:26:14.020 --> 00:26:14.890
Well we could.

00:26:14.890 --> 00:26:17.080
But if you don't
have the ability

00:26:17.080 --> 00:26:21.000
to divide the work of a
live-running pipeline,

00:26:21.000 --> 00:26:23.860
your additional
workers will be idle.

00:26:23.860 --> 00:26:25.990
There will be nothing
for them to do.

00:26:25.990 --> 00:26:29.710
So without this ability
to dynamically reassign

00:26:29.710 --> 00:26:35.750
work and re-balance work,
autoscaling just doesn't work.

00:26:35.750 --> 00:26:36.730
And here's a diagram.

00:26:36.730 --> 00:26:41.260
Here is a screenshot of another
real-life pipeline, which

00:26:41.260 --> 00:26:45.100
starts with three
workers and kind of keeps

00:26:45.100 --> 00:26:49.800
learning about the properties
of your input data.

00:26:49.800 --> 00:26:52.150
And realizes we can process--

00:26:52.150 --> 00:26:54.100
we can process the
pipeline faster

00:26:54.100 --> 00:26:56.230
if we added more workers.

00:26:56.230 --> 00:26:58.980
And its great that we have the
ability to divide the work,

00:26:58.980 --> 00:27:00.070
so we launch new workers.

00:27:00.070 --> 00:27:02.350
We allocate work to them.

00:27:02.350 --> 00:27:05.620
Every worker is working hard,
there is no white space.

00:27:05.620 --> 00:27:08.440
They are fully utilized.

00:27:08.440 --> 00:27:12.130
Eventually, a pipeline
reaches thousands of workers.

00:27:12.130 --> 00:27:13.825
It crunches the
data and completes

00:27:13.825 --> 00:27:18.190
and then scales
down, shuts down.

00:27:18.190 --> 00:27:23.270
Let me show you how exactly
autoscaling is benefiting you

00:27:23.270 --> 00:27:27.726
on the example of my pipeline
that I just recently launched.

00:27:27.726 --> 00:27:28.730
Demo machines please.

00:27:48.430 --> 00:27:50.200
All right.

00:27:50.200 --> 00:27:53.430
So here is my pipeline,
back to my pipeline.

00:27:53.430 --> 00:28:01.040
It completed within
about eight minutes.

00:28:03.770 --> 00:28:05.270
The reason why I
know it's completed

00:28:05.270 --> 00:28:09.350
is the green check boxes in each
of the transformation steps,

00:28:09.350 --> 00:28:10.820
for those of you
who haven't seen

00:28:10.820 --> 00:28:13.270
the Dataflow console before.

00:28:13.270 --> 00:28:16.910
One interesting feature that
we have in the Google Console--

00:28:16.910 --> 00:28:19.850
Google Dataflow
console-- is the history

00:28:19.850 --> 00:28:21.970
of autoscaling decisions.

00:28:21.970 --> 00:28:23.760
So check it out
when you go back,

00:28:23.760 --> 00:28:26.260
if you haven't seen it yet.

00:28:26.260 --> 00:28:31.410
Here's the full history of
what went on in your pipeline.

00:28:31.410 --> 00:28:35.960
Read from the bottom-- read
from the bottom of the screen.

00:28:35.960 --> 00:28:39.500
We started with a pool
of a single worker.

00:28:39.500 --> 00:28:42.020
And remember, I
had about 40 files

00:28:42.020 --> 00:28:46.600
in my data set of
approximately the same size,

00:28:46.600 --> 00:28:50.590
with the exception of one file
which was 50 times longer--

00:28:50.590 --> 00:28:54.830
50 times larger-- than
all the other files.

00:28:54.830 --> 00:28:58.860
So the data flow kept
re-estimating and reallocating

00:28:58.860 --> 00:28:59.360
workers.

00:28:59.360 --> 00:29:03.670
It increased the number of
workers to two, then 8, to 15.

00:29:03.670 --> 00:29:07.840
And 15 seemed to be a good
balance of number of workers

00:29:07.840 --> 00:29:10.300
and available data inputs.

00:29:10.300 --> 00:29:13.690
So we kept working at that
level until we process all

00:29:13.690 --> 00:29:15.970
the records and then shut down.

00:29:18.870 --> 00:29:22.560
Hopefully this was a
useful demonstration

00:29:22.560 --> 00:29:25.400
of the capabilities of
dynamic load rebalancing

00:29:25.400 --> 00:29:27.200
and autoscaling.

00:29:27.200 --> 00:29:28.950
And with this I
would like to invite

00:29:28.950 --> 00:29:33.570
Ankur Chauhan from Brightcove
to talk about how Brightcove is

00:29:33.570 --> 00:29:37.860
using Dataflow at Brightcove.

00:29:37.860 --> 00:29:39.410
Can we go back to
the slides please?

00:29:45.290 --> 00:29:46.780
ANKUR CHAUHAN: Thanks Sergei.

00:29:46.780 --> 00:29:47.560
Hello everyone.

00:29:47.560 --> 00:29:52.200
I am-- hello everyone.

00:29:52.200 --> 00:29:54.390
I'm Ankur Chauhan
from Brightcove.

00:29:54.390 --> 00:29:58.410
I am a software engineer with
the video analytics team.

00:29:58.410 --> 00:30:04.410
Brightcove is an online video
platform which provides video--

00:30:04.410 --> 00:30:10.569
end-to-end video services to
companies large and small.

00:30:10.569 --> 00:30:12.360
They're supposed to be
Snapchat over there,

00:30:12.360 --> 00:30:14.700
but it kept on disappearing
after 10 seconds.

00:30:14.700 --> 00:30:17.470
[LAUGHTER]

00:30:17.470 --> 00:30:23.770
So we provide video analytics
to companies big and small,

00:30:23.770 --> 00:30:26.940
which means that we
handle lots of data.

00:30:26.940 --> 00:30:32.640
We deliver about 8,500
years of video every month,

00:30:32.640 --> 00:30:36.210
which generates about
seven billion events a day.

00:30:36.210 --> 00:30:39.540
Which generates a
lot of time series.

00:30:39.540 --> 00:30:43.140
But because we believe
that raw data just

00:30:43.140 --> 00:30:46.110
crunched up together and
summed up is not useful,

00:30:46.110 --> 00:30:51.480
we aggregate it down to about
80 million unique time series.

00:30:51.480 --> 00:30:56.560
And all this is maintained by a
pretty small team of engineers,

00:30:56.560 --> 00:30:59.700
about five or six of us.

00:30:59.700 --> 00:31:03.800
So basically half
of the team is here.

00:31:03.800 --> 00:31:07.940
So as Sergei pointed out, we
followed a pretty traditional

00:31:07.940 --> 00:31:08.510
approach.

00:31:08.510 --> 00:31:11.340
We went with a lambda model.

00:31:11.340 --> 00:31:14.330
So as you can see,
the top part is

00:31:14.330 --> 00:31:16.660
optimized for
streaming calculation,

00:31:16.660 --> 00:31:20.510
whereas the lower part is
more of a batch system.

00:31:20.510 --> 00:31:23.870
Events come in through the
top from our web players

00:31:23.870 --> 00:31:28.730
and Roku devices and
Android devices and iPhones.

00:31:28.730 --> 00:31:32.630
And they come to our
beacon over SCDP,

00:31:32.630 --> 00:31:35.150
get aggregated in real
time and stored away

00:31:35.150 --> 00:31:38.990
into the real-time database.

00:31:38.990 --> 00:31:40.790
At the same time,
we write all of this

00:31:40.790 --> 00:31:42.890
down into logs and store--

00:31:42.890 --> 00:31:46.820
and store it to S3 so
that we can aggregate it

00:31:46.820 --> 00:31:48.590
every few hours.

00:31:48.590 --> 00:31:53.840
Because one of the deals that
you make with the lambda model

00:31:53.840 --> 00:31:55.880
is that the streaming
system is really

00:31:55.880 --> 00:31:58.610
optimized for, well,
being real-time.

00:31:58.610 --> 00:32:02.090
And that means that it is lossy.

00:32:02.090 --> 00:32:05.090
So to take care of that, we
have a reconciled database

00:32:05.090 --> 00:32:08.480
which goes in every few hours
and happily churns through all

00:32:08.480 --> 00:32:12.740
the logs and gives it to
a reconciled database.

00:32:12.740 --> 00:32:19.270
And the API comes along for
every API request and says,

00:32:19.270 --> 00:32:22.610
hey, give me the
data for this request

00:32:22.610 --> 00:32:24.140
that the user is trying to make.

00:32:24.140 --> 00:32:29.010
It merges the two data sets
and gives it to the user.

00:32:29.010 --> 00:32:33.540
On the side we have
a red-shift database,

00:32:33.540 --> 00:32:38.040
and over that we have
some BI tools written.

00:32:38.040 --> 00:32:45.270
So if anyone knows the work
that goes into managing a lambda

00:32:45.270 --> 00:32:47.730
model system and
everything, they

00:32:47.730 --> 00:32:50.550
would know that it's
a little latent.

00:32:50.550 --> 00:32:53.430
Hadoop takes a lot of time
to churn through data.

00:32:53.430 --> 00:32:57.690
You don't absolutely get all
your results immediately.

00:32:57.690 --> 00:33:00.720
Plus, there are more or
less two code bases here.

00:33:00.720 --> 00:33:05.950
So every so often we would get
a support ticket which says,

00:33:05.950 --> 00:33:09.210
hey, my result from yesterday
doesn't match the result

00:33:09.210 --> 00:33:09.780
from today.

00:33:09.780 --> 00:33:13.030
Or, why is this ever
so slightly different?

00:33:13.030 --> 00:33:17.170
So there is always
pain for that.

00:33:17.170 --> 00:33:22.300
So all this is working fine,
and for many years it did.

00:33:22.300 --> 00:33:26.850
And then things happen.

00:33:26.850 --> 00:33:29.570
The most important
one is Brightcove

00:33:29.570 --> 00:33:31.320
is a growing company.

00:33:31.320 --> 00:33:35.310
And more people come online and
they watch more video, which

00:33:35.310 --> 00:33:37.650
means that we have more scale.

00:33:37.650 --> 00:33:39.390
We are adding more disks.

00:33:39.390 --> 00:33:43.740
We are adding more compute nodes
to our Hadoop cluster, which

00:33:43.740 --> 00:33:45.840
is now bursting at its seams.

00:33:45.840 --> 00:33:49.170
So we are spending
more and more time

00:33:49.170 --> 00:33:53.180
just keeping the
lights on, essentially,

00:33:53.180 --> 00:33:56.820
which means that lesser and
lesser features get delivered.

00:33:56.820 --> 00:34:00.440
So we had to do
something about that.

00:34:00.440 --> 00:34:02.690
But even more
important is that there

00:34:02.690 --> 00:34:06.200
were some new features
in line, specifically

00:34:06.200 --> 00:34:11.360
session collation, which we
thought was really important.

00:34:11.360 --> 00:34:13.730
Session collation
basically means

00:34:13.730 --> 00:34:17.780
that we had to look at a
viewing session in its entirety.

00:34:17.780 --> 00:34:21.110
It's a much more
resource-intensive thing

00:34:21.110 --> 00:34:24.739
to do and Hadoop and the
home-grown streaming system

00:34:24.739 --> 00:34:26.570
was just not cutting it.

00:34:26.570 --> 00:34:30.800
It was a lot of work and we
wanted to get something better.

00:34:30.800 --> 00:34:35.239
At the same time, we wanted to
use a more performing execution

00:34:35.239 --> 00:34:38.239
engine which could support
all this and the future things

00:34:38.239 --> 00:34:39.830
that we wanted to do.

00:34:39.830 --> 00:34:45.254
As well as a more robust and
performance database layer

00:34:45.254 --> 00:34:46.670
which could take
care of all this.

00:34:49.840 --> 00:34:53.380
So we went, we did our research.

00:34:53.380 --> 00:34:56.409
And we found that everyone
seemed to recommend

00:34:56.409 --> 00:34:59.185
these slew of technologies.

00:34:59.185 --> 00:35:00.700
They're pretty
amazing technologies.

00:35:00.700 --> 00:35:02.890
They are open source,
so you can actually

00:35:02.890 --> 00:35:05.590
see the source of
the product, and you

00:35:05.590 --> 00:35:07.810
can figure out whether
they actually wrote

00:35:07.810 --> 00:35:09.130
the tests that they did.

00:35:13.090 --> 00:35:13.970
They are performing.

00:35:13.970 --> 00:35:16.350
They have big
communities behind them.

00:35:16.350 --> 00:35:18.880
So you know that
if you get stuck

00:35:18.880 --> 00:35:20.960
you will have some
one to turn to,

00:35:20.960 --> 00:35:24.560
and hopefully they will be
able to answer your questions.

00:35:24.560 --> 00:35:29.430
So Kafka, Spark, Cassandra,
Hadoop, Mesos, all good.

00:35:29.430 --> 00:35:33.320
And we were like, Yeah,
lets do this, sit down.

00:35:33.320 --> 00:35:34.760
So we started planning.

00:35:34.760 --> 00:35:38.480
And within the first
15 minutes of planning,

00:35:38.480 --> 00:35:41.210
you know, beep, beep, beep.

00:35:41.210 --> 00:35:43.580
Pagers going off in our heads.

00:35:43.580 --> 00:35:45.980
This is a lot of
systems to manage.

00:35:45.980 --> 00:35:48.290
And with five people,
we would essentially

00:35:48.290 --> 00:35:51.710
be just managing these systems.

00:35:51.710 --> 00:35:54.940
And we had to, first
of all, become experts

00:35:54.940 --> 00:35:55.970
at these systems.

00:35:55.970 --> 00:36:00.350
To run them, establish them,
build monitoring resources

00:36:00.350 --> 00:36:02.690
for them-- so
dashboards and alerts.

00:36:02.690 --> 00:36:09.220
And then each of these systems
have like 500 variables

00:36:09.220 --> 00:36:12.470
to tune just right
to get them working

00:36:12.470 --> 00:36:14.750
in the right way we want them.

00:36:14.750 --> 00:36:16.010
So it's not easy.

00:36:16.010 --> 00:36:23.860
So we were like, OK, need to
step back, look at this again.

00:36:23.860 --> 00:36:28.570
So the basic theory in this
architecture is very sound.

00:36:28.570 --> 00:36:31.930
You have a simple thing
that you're trying to do.

00:36:31.930 --> 00:36:34.660
Event collector goes
to a message queue

00:36:34.660 --> 00:36:37.240
which goes to some
computation thing

00:36:37.240 --> 00:36:39.700
and gets written to a database.

00:36:39.700 --> 00:36:43.090
And you want to write
it to a warehouse

00:36:43.090 --> 00:36:45.160
and finally serve it out.

00:36:45.160 --> 00:36:47.530
But each of these
components are very heavy,

00:36:47.530 --> 00:36:51.960
so we wanted to sort
of make them lighter.

00:36:51.960 --> 00:36:54.940
And what we wanted
to do was look

00:36:54.940 --> 00:36:56.860
for some managed
solutions for this,

00:36:56.860 --> 00:36:59.020
for each part of the system.

00:36:59.020 --> 00:37:01.450
So that with the
five-person team

00:37:01.450 --> 00:37:04.600
we actually manage
less and do more.

00:37:07.200 --> 00:37:08.370
So.

00:37:08.370 --> 00:37:11.650
Looked around, and
lots of claims,

00:37:11.650 --> 00:37:12.960
lots of marketing material.

00:37:12.960 --> 00:37:14.880
But if you're an
engineer, you're

00:37:14.880 --> 00:37:17.310
taught one thing for sure--

00:37:17.310 --> 00:37:18.200
trust but verify.

00:37:18.200 --> 00:37:21.150
Never trust a marketing
material or a blog post

00:37:21.150 --> 00:37:25.560
just because it says 100%
managed and performant.

00:37:25.560 --> 00:37:30.630
So we went out and we wrote
lots of performance tests.

00:37:30.630 --> 00:37:33.030
We did our due
diligence in making sure

00:37:33.030 --> 00:37:37.170
that we could swap every
component individually

00:37:37.170 --> 00:37:41.640
without sort of getting
locked into the wrong thing.

00:37:41.640 --> 00:37:46.510
And we went step-by-step to
make sure that we got it right.

00:37:46.510 --> 00:37:50.890
So easy pass-- easy parts first.

00:37:50.890 --> 00:37:55.980
We saw Google and we saw
Google Container Engine.

00:37:55.980 --> 00:38:01.200
Best way to deploy applications,
so we went with it.

00:38:01.200 --> 00:38:07.180
We deployed our micro services
for the API on top of GKE.

00:38:07.180 --> 00:38:08.080
Straightforward.

00:38:08.080 --> 00:38:09.480
We knew how to do it.

00:38:09.480 --> 00:38:13.680
Run some-- there are lots
of resources to help us out,

00:38:13.680 --> 00:38:17.100
and it's Kubernetes,
so even more community.

00:38:17.100 --> 00:38:20.280
So that was perfect.

00:38:20.280 --> 00:38:24.560
Next we went with how
to deal with Kafka.

00:38:24.560 --> 00:38:27.720
Kafka has this magical
dependency called Zookeeper.

00:38:27.720 --> 00:38:31.500
Which if you have
managed it, it's

00:38:31.500 --> 00:38:34.350
just completely magical in
terms of just going down

00:38:34.350 --> 00:38:35.880
for no apparent reason.

00:38:39.200 --> 00:38:42.040
So we didn't want
to deal with it.

00:38:42.040 --> 00:38:44.980
And what we found
was that Pub/Sub was

00:38:44.980 --> 00:38:47.290
a very good replacement for it.

00:38:47.290 --> 00:38:52.440
It is a globally available
and durable message queue.

00:38:52.440 --> 00:38:54.390
Which means that all
the work that we would

00:38:54.390 --> 00:38:57.480
have to do in terms of
keeping replicas alive

00:38:57.480 --> 00:39:00.510
and making sure the
Kafka is properly

00:39:00.510 --> 00:39:04.810
provisioned for our scale and is
all good, we don't have to do.

00:39:04.810 --> 00:39:08.190
So three months saved.

00:39:08.190 --> 00:39:09.960
Message de-duplication by ID.

00:39:09.960 --> 00:39:16.440
So you can, with the Pub/Sub
and Dataflow working together,

00:39:16.440 --> 00:39:21.000
you can actually deliver
messages again and again

00:39:21.000 --> 00:39:22.650
and assign them a unique ID.

00:39:22.650 --> 00:39:26.190
And Dataflow will make sure
that it processes that message

00:39:26.190 --> 00:39:27.230
just once.

00:39:27.230 --> 00:39:31.080
So exactly once.

00:39:31.080 --> 00:39:36.330
And, finally, because
it's globally available,

00:39:36.330 --> 00:39:38.520
very little chance that
you get disconnected

00:39:38.520 --> 00:39:43.650
from every single region of
Google everywhere in the world.

00:39:43.650 --> 00:39:45.680
Pretty much not going to happen.

00:39:45.680 --> 00:39:48.860
So great.

00:39:48.860 --> 00:39:51.560
Next thing that we looked
at was our database

00:39:51.560 --> 00:39:57.980
it's the single most pinpoint
operational burden of our team.

00:39:57.980 --> 00:40:03.920
We have spent weeks, sometimes,
just bringing the system up.

00:40:03.920 --> 00:40:06.860
So we wanted to find
a solution to it.

00:40:06.860 --> 00:40:09.710
Our data set is
time series based.

00:40:09.710 --> 00:40:15.290
So Bigtable-- Cassandra
was our first choice.

00:40:15.290 --> 00:40:18.360
And then we looked around and
we saw Bigtable was there.

00:40:18.360 --> 00:40:20.270
And if you read
the Cassandra docs,

00:40:20.270 --> 00:40:22.400
one of the inspirations
for Cassandra

00:40:22.400 --> 00:40:24.590
is the Bigtable table.

00:40:24.590 --> 00:40:27.950
So we were like, OK, Google
knows what it's doing.

00:40:27.950 --> 00:40:32.660
It's a pretty well-respected
people, so let's check it out.

00:40:32.660 --> 00:40:35.510
We wrote our performance
tests and it just

00:40:35.510 --> 00:40:39.170
blew past everything
that we thought.

00:40:39.170 --> 00:40:44.160
In addition to that, it's
one button scale up and down.

00:40:44.160 --> 00:40:46.020
So go into the console.

00:40:46.020 --> 00:40:51.330
You said the number of nodes
to 30 and save and it's done.

00:40:51.330 --> 00:40:54.420
And it provides
the interface using

00:40:54.420 --> 00:40:59.400
the good old Edge-based APIs, so
there's lots of tooling for it

00:40:59.400 --> 00:41:00.780
and it works very well.

00:41:00.780 --> 00:41:04.610
Very reliable and performant.

00:41:04.610 --> 00:41:06.200
So database, another one.

00:41:06.200 --> 00:41:07.200
It's the easy button.

00:41:10.010 --> 00:41:17.480
And finally ad hoc analysis
and doing [INAUDIBLE] things.

00:41:17.480 --> 00:41:21.440
At our scale, we get
terabytes of data every day.

00:41:21.440 --> 00:41:27.510
So sooner or later a support
call will come in which says,

00:41:27.510 --> 00:41:32.160
hey, can you find this absurd
thing from your data set?

00:41:32.160 --> 00:41:35.430
And we wanted to
answer that question.

00:41:35.430 --> 00:41:37.640
And in the past, whenever
we had to do that

00:41:37.640 --> 00:41:41.120
it was a couple of
days of going to S3

00:41:41.120 --> 00:41:43.520
and downloading a
whole bunch of logs,

00:41:43.520 --> 00:41:47.000
running it through grep or
some other tool that we wrote,

00:41:47.000 --> 00:41:48.001
and giving the answer.

00:41:50.950 --> 00:41:52.860
If you have used
BigQuery, you'll

00:41:52.860 --> 00:41:56.670
know how easy it is to
actually run queries in it.

00:41:56.670 --> 00:42:00.570
It's a console, you write
the SQL query and it's done.

00:42:00.570 --> 00:42:05.190
It's almost as cheap as
just storing files in S3.

00:42:05.190 --> 00:42:08.180
And it's completely
managed and it

00:42:08.180 --> 00:42:10.100
churns through
terabytes in seconds,

00:42:10.100 --> 00:42:12.690
so immediate win for us.

00:42:12.690 --> 00:42:16.590
Things that used to take days
for us to debug and diagnose

00:42:16.590 --> 00:42:19.600
took maybe five
minutes, at best.

00:42:23.360 --> 00:42:24.590
Elephant in the room.

00:42:24.590 --> 00:42:27.920
Spark and Hadoop.

00:42:27.920 --> 00:42:30.770
It's a monster system
because it's trying

00:42:30.770 --> 00:42:32.330
to solve a monster problem.

00:42:32.330 --> 00:42:34.790
But it's something that
we wanted to tackle.

00:42:34.790 --> 00:42:39.500
So we again looked around,
and just at this time

00:42:39.500 --> 00:42:42.360
Google Dataflow had
come out of beta.

00:42:42.360 --> 00:42:46.230
So-- sorry, come into beta.

00:42:46.230 --> 00:42:50.650
So we looked at it, we read
the paper that was published.

00:42:50.650 --> 00:42:53.580
We read some blog posts
and we read some docs

00:42:53.580 --> 00:42:55.740
and we liked every single thing.

00:42:55.740 --> 00:43:00.180
It was right there, and
we trusted none of it.

00:43:00.180 --> 00:43:05.910
Followed by, we went in, and
we wrote a proof of concept

00:43:05.910 --> 00:43:07.650
over a hackathon.

00:43:07.650 --> 00:43:09.450
And two days later,
we were like--

00:43:09.450 --> 00:43:13.930
50% of our feature set
was implemented in it.

00:43:13.930 --> 00:43:16.780
Which was great.

00:43:16.780 --> 00:43:22.140
So we sat down and
looked at it even more.

00:43:22.140 --> 00:43:26.150
For the first time, I think
it provides a unified batch

00:43:26.150 --> 00:43:28.240
and streaming model.

00:43:28.240 --> 00:43:30.180
The Beam model is very powerful.

00:43:30.180 --> 00:43:35.850
So you don't have to maintain
two almost identical code

00:43:35.850 --> 00:43:40.580
bases just to do the same
thing, which is a big win.

00:43:40.580 --> 00:43:43.170
Streaming reconciliation,
which means

00:43:43.170 --> 00:43:48.120
that whenever a message
gets picked up off Pub/Sub,

00:43:48.120 --> 00:43:50.340
you know that it's
going to get written.

00:43:50.340 --> 00:43:52.740
It's guaranteed to get done.

00:43:52.740 --> 00:43:57.300
And you don't have to do
anything of the sort of retries

00:43:57.300 --> 00:43:59.550
and all of those things.

00:43:59.550 --> 00:44:02.040
And finally, its
batteries included.

00:44:02.040 --> 00:44:04.500
What I mean by that is
it comes with a slew

00:44:04.500 --> 00:44:07.950
of inputs and outputs,
of sources and sinks.

00:44:07.950 --> 00:44:09.800
So you don't have
to implement them.

00:44:09.800 --> 00:44:13.310
Those is text input,
which Sergei used.

00:44:13.310 --> 00:44:16.920
There's Pub/Sub, and on the
output side we use Bigtable,

00:44:16.920 --> 00:44:21.420
but there's BigQuery,
text output, many others.

00:44:21.420 --> 00:44:23.200
And lots of windowing operators.

00:44:23.200 --> 00:44:27.120
So, we want to do
session collation,

00:44:27.120 --> 00:44:32.350
we have to essentially say
window by session, and done.

00:44:32.350 --> 00:44:35.790
So, immediate win for
us and we were already

00:44:35.790 --> 00:44:43.040
one year ahead in our game of
implementing all this stuff.

00:44:43.040 --> 00:44:44.360
Which just great.

00:44:44.360 --> 00:44:48.770
So putting it back into our
original architecture diagram.

00:44:48.770 --> 00:44:51.770
Everything inside the
dotted box over there

00:44:51.770 --> 00:44:53.480
is a managed service.

00:44:53.480 --> 00:44:55.280
What that means is
that we would never

00:44:55.280 --> 00:44:57.930
get a page for those services.

00:44:57.930 --> 00:44:59.980
Which also means that
someone in Google,

00:44:59.980 --> 00:45:03.500
who is very well
trained for that job,

00:45:03.500 --> 00:45:07.130
knows how to diagnose, debug,
and fix those services when

00:45:07.130 --> 00:45:09.170
they do go wrong.

00:45:09.170 --> 00:45:11.030
And it's a much
more linear system,

00:45:11.030 --> 00:45:14.240
which means that when
things are not working

00:45:14.240 --> 00:45:16.010
or when we want to
develop something,

00:45:16.010 --> 00:45:17.910
we know exactly
where to point at.

00:45:17.910 --> 00:45:20.430
We know what to tweak.

00:45:20.430 --> 00:45:22.550
We know how to reason
about it, which

00:45:22.550 --> 00:45:26.540
makes everyone's life easier.

00:45:26.540 --> 00:45:29.420
So what did this give us?

00:45:29.420 --> 00:45:32.090
Less pain, more features.

00:45:32.090 --> 00:45:33.500
Faster.

00:45:33.500 --> 00:45:35.420
And if you look on
the side, we now

00:45:35.420 --> 00:45:40.620
have another box which is BI
tools and ad hoc Analysis.

00:45:40.620 --> 00:45:43.490
So we got more stuff
done because we

00:45:43.490 --> 00:45:46.880
were able to get access
to tools which had

00:45:46.880 --> 00:45:49.970
much lower marginal cost to us.

00:45:49.970 --> 00:45:52.280
There's no thinking
around, this query

00:45:52.280 --> 00:45:55.900
is going to take eight days
to run, so let me not do it.

00:45:55.900 --> 00:45:59.240
You just go in, run the
BigQuery query, get your answer,

00:45:59.240 --> 00:46:02.840
get on with life.

00:46:02.840 --> 00:46:06.650
All of this took about six
months to sort of perfect

00:46:06.650 --> 00:46:08.690
and get it right.

00:46:08.690 --> 00:46:10.580
There were lots of
lessons learned.

00:46:10.580 --> 00:46:17.570
And if some of you have managed
Hadoop, a lot of that knowledge

00:46:17.570 --> 00:46:19.790
transfers over.

00:46:19.790 --> 00:46:23.570
Specifically, if
you want to speed up

00:46:23.570 --> 00:46:26.330
your data flow or
your processing,

00:46:26.330 --> 00:46:28.850
the best way to do it
is process less data.

00:46:28.850 --> 00:46:33.670
Reduce your I/O. Use a better
desk, namely a SSD disk.

00:46:33.670 --> 00:46:34.790
They are much faster.

00:46:34.790 --> 00:46:39.790
You'll get a performance
boost without doing anything.

00:46:39.790 --> 00:46:46.010
Secondly is use a schema
data interchange format.

00:46:46.010 --> 00:46:48.160
Protobuf is a great one.

00:46:48.160 --> 00:46:50.170
We started with JSON.

00:46:50.170 --> 00:46:52.420
JSON is great when
you're just starting out.

00:46:52.420 --> 00:46:55.240
It lets you tweak and
twiddle with stuff,

00:46:55.240 --> 00:46:57.850
but it's not optimized
for machines.

00:46:57.850 --> 00:47:00.220
That means a lot of your
compute is just spent

00:47:00.220 --> 00:47:02.620
serializing-deserializing.

00:47:02.620 --> 00:47:06.460
And in addition to that, you
would spend a lot of time

00:47:06.460 --> 00:47:08.450
just cleaning up the data.

00:47:08.450 --> 00:47:10.960
So you'll write
tons of code just

00:47:10.960 --> 00:47:13.180
to make sure that the
right fields are present,

00:47:13.180 --> 00:47:15.790
the right format is
there, or something

00:47:15.790 --> 00:47:18.280
or the other in the same thing.

00:47:18.280 --> 00:47:22.720
So we use protobuf and we
like it and it provides

00:47:22.720 --> 00:47:27.160
many, many features, some of
them being it is efficient

00:47:27.160 --> 00:47:30.840
and backwards compatibility.

00:47:30.840 --> 00:47:35.370
Finally, being from an
analytics background,

00:47:35.370 --> 00:47:38.610
I cannot stress this
enough-- monitor everything.

00:47:38.610 --> 00:47:41.190
What makes sense
for our workload,

00:47:41.190 --> 00:47:44.820
CPU, and Pub/Sub backlog may
not make sense for yours.

00:47:44.820 --> 00:47:47.520
You might have a completely
different set of metrics

00:47:47.520 --> 00:47:49.050
which makes sense to you.

00:47:49.050 --> 00:47:51.090
So monitor them.

00:47:51.090 --> 00:47:53.400
We make lots of
dashboards so that we

00:47:53.400 --> 00:47:56.970
have visibility into
everything, and that's

00:47:56.970 --> 00:47:58.560
worked out very well for us.

00:47:58.560 --> 00:48:02.840
And finally, when all
fails, you get stuck.

00:48:02.840 --> 00:48:05.730
There is a data
flow team, which was

00:48:05.730 --> 00:48:09.480
very eager to help us out in--

00:48:09.480 --> 00:48:11.290
on every turn.

00:48:11.290 --> 00:48:12.900
So we really liked the team.

00:48:15.880 --> 00:48:19.950
And then, before I go
into a short little demo,

00:48:19.950 --> 00:48:24.200
this is a future roadmap which
I would not be showing you here

00:48:24.200 --> 00:48:30.080
at all had we been implementing
all this stuff using

00:48:30.080 --> 00:48:32.300
Spark and Hadoop.

00:48:32.300 --> 00:48:33.580
Adaptive aggregates.

00:48:33.580 --> 00:48:36.500
So we analyze our
customers' request

00:48:36.500 --> 00:48:39.140
volume and their
request patterns

00:48:39.140 --> 00:48:42.530
and automatically adjust
how we aggregate our data.

00:48:42.530 --> 00:48:46.820
So their queries
magically become faster.

00:48:46.820 --> 00:48:49.310
Lot more BI tools.

00:48:49.310 --> 00:48:51.950
We have started to build
a lot more BI tools on top

00:48:51.950 --> 00:48:55.210
of BigQuery, which give us--

00:48:55.210 --> 00:48:57.230
which give our
internal customers

00:48:57.230 --> 00:49:01.610
much more visibility into
data which just wasn't there.

00:49:01.610 --> 00:49:04.295
And finally, and it's
a big one, that we

00:49:04.295 --> 00:49:08.180
are able to unify our analytics
and billing pipelines.

00:49:08.180 --> 00:49:10.940
Because there is
a hard guarantee

00:49:10.940 --> 00:49:14.990
that your data, once it's
acknowledged, actually gets

00:49:14.990 --> 00:49:16.550
saved to our database.

00:49:16.550 --> 00:49:19.820
We can be sure that
it's good enough--

00:49:19.820 --> 00:49:22.980
good enough that
that data exists.

00:49:22.980 --> 00:49:29.040
So the billing system can trust
this data analytics system.

00:49:29.040 --> 00:49:31.410
Our previous analytics
system always had this thing

00:49:31.410 --> 00:49:36.910
that the billing data was
ever so slightly different.

00:49:36.910 --> 00:49:39.300
So we would trust
the billing and there

00:49:39.300 --> 00:49:41.070
would be questions
about it, like, why

00:49:41.070 --> 00:49:42.360
is it slightly different?

00:49:42.360 --> 00:49:43.800
And we would have to explain.

00:49:43.800 --> 00:49:45.150
No longer.

00:49:45.150 --> 00:49:46.120
So that's great.

00:49:49.670 --> 00:49:50.940
Everyone's happy.

00:49:50.940 --> 00:49:54.380
Everyone is joyous.

00:49:54.380 --> 00:49:58.340
So, with that, I'll show you
what our pipeline actually

00:49:58.340 --> 00:49:59.540
looks like.

00:49:59.540 --> 00:50:06.110
This is-- I'm going to cheat
a little bit because demo gods

00:50:06.110 --> 00:50:11.400
are never great during demos.

00:50:11.400 --> 00:50:16.010
So this is a video of the
same Google Cloud Console

00:50:16.010 --> 00:50:18.780
of our production pipeline.

00:50:18.780 --> 00:50:21.690
As you can see, we are reading
from two Pub/Sub topics

00:50:21.690 --> 00:50:26.400
at the top, performing a
bunch of computations on them,

00:50:26.400 --> 00:50:29.580
and churning through
data constantly.

00:50:29.580 --> 00:50:36.300
And then finally writing
it back to do Bigtable.

00:50:36.300 --> 00:50:39.390
The amazing thing here
for me is that it's

00:50:39.390 --> 00:50:44.570
a very simple representation
of a complex piece of software.

00:50:44.570 --> 00:50:47.870
Each box directly lines
up with a piece of code

00:50:47.870 --> 00:50:49.130
that we have written.

00:50:49.130 --> 00:50:52.640
So even though data flow
underneath the covers

00:50:52.640 --> 00:50:57.140
is performing all these
optimizations of--

00:50:57.140 --> 00:50:58.970
all different sorts
of optimizations,

00:50:58.970 --> 00:51:00.670
we don't have to do those.

00:51:00.670 --> 00:51:03.260
And that's amazing.

00:51:03.260 --> 00:51:07.950
And we can just concentrate
on how things look like.

00:51:07.950 --> 00:51:12.650
So that was how our
pipeline looks like.

00:51:12.650 --> 00:51:15.670
And next we have--

00:51:21.400 --> 00:51:24.520
next we have the
monitoring dashboard.

00:51:24.520 --> 00:51:29.470
So our main intuition
behind making this dashboard

00:51:29.470 --> 00:51:31.840
is keeping it simple.

00:51:31.840 --> 00:51:35.170
So that when you wake up at
3:00 AM because of a page--

00:51:35.170 --> 00:51:39.370
first of all, the block color
does not hurt your eyes.

00:51:39.370 --> 00:51:42.610
The second part
is that everything

00:51:42.610 --> 00:51:47.630
is exactly what you need.

00:51:47.630 --> 00:51:51.730
And so the first row
is Pub/Sub metrics.

00:51:51.730 --> 00:51:54.940
For us, Pub/Sub backlog
is a good indicator

00:51:54.940 --> 00:51:55.970
of how we're doing.

00:51:55.970 --> 00:51:58.990
So when, you know,
there's a sudden increase

00:51:58.990 --> 00:52:04.880
in messages published to us, and
data flow starts falling behind

00:52:04.880 --> 00:52:08.020
and it doesn't keep
up for some threshold,

00:52:08.020 --> 00:52:09.940
let's say three hours.

00:52:09.940 --> 00:52:13.615
We get an alert saying,
hey, go look at Pub/Sub.

00:52:13.615 --> 00:52:17.200
Or go look at the Dataflow
it's not keeping up.

00:52:17.200 --> 00:52:21.770
We come here and we see that,
OK, message volume increased.

00:52:21.770 --> 00:52:25.930
Pub/Sub is backed up
because Dataflow is churning

00:52:25.930 --> 00:52:27.790
through it, it'll be good.

00:52:27.790 --> 00:52:29.962
Just leave it.

00:52:29.962 --> 00:52:33.910
If it is auto-scaled, it will
scale up, process everything,

00:52:33.910 --> 00:52:34.770
scale down.

00:52:34.770 --> 00:52:36.370
Everyone's fine.

00:52:36.370 --> 00:52:39.610
The CPU utilization
we monitor that just

00:52:39.610 --> 00:52:42.550
to make sure that we know that
we are spending the exact right

00:52:42.550 --> 00:52:45.280
amount of money.

00:52:45.280 --> 00:52:49.330
We want to keep these machines
as utilized as possible,

00:52:49.330 --> 00:52:53.530
but not so much that
they are all overwhelmed.

00:52:53.530 --> 00:52:56.110
And low CPU is generally
a good indicator

00:52:56.110 --> 00:53:00.790
that the pipeline just
seized up for some reason,

00:53:00.790 --> 00:53:02.740
needs some more work.

00:53:02.740 --> 00:53:06.430
So, to the point,
monitoring that

00:53:06.430 --> 00:53:10.560
helps you go back to sleep.

00:53:10.560 --> 00:53:17.600
And with that, I will hand
the mike back to Sergei.

00:53:17.600 --> 00:53:24.730
[APPLAUSE]

00:53:26.494 --> 00:53:29.250
SERGEI SOKOLENKO: Thanks, Ankur.

00:53:29.250 --> 00:53:30.620
So let's recap.

00:53:30.620 --> 00:53:34.180
We took a look at how easy it
is to launch Dataflow pipelines

00:53:34.180 --> 00:53:36.365
using Dataflow Templates.

00:53:36.365 --> 00:53:39.940
We deep dived into two
specific technical benefits

00:53:39.940 --> 00:53:45.610
of Cloud Dataflow-- dynamic load
rebalancing and auto-scaling.

00:53:45.610 --> 00:53:48.760
And we also heard
from Brightcove.

00:53:48.760 --> 00:53:50.860
How easy it was to
build a video analytics

00:53:50.860 --> 00:53:54.460
solution using Dataflow.

00:53:54.460 --> 00:53:58.410
[MUSIC PLAYING]

